# 7. REAL YOLO ROAD DAMAGE DETECTION

This is one of the most important components.

Implement genuine YOLO inference.

The preferred road damage categories are:

- D00 — Longitudinal crack
- D10 — Transverse crack
- D20 — Alligator crack
- D40 — Pothole

Use a verified, compatible road damage model or train a custom YOLO model using RDD2022.

Primary dataset:

Road Damage Dataset 2022 (RDD2022)

Official repository:
https://github.com/sekilab/RoadDamageDetector

Dataset information:
https://crddc2022.sekilab.global/data/

Use official dataset annotations and clearly document their licensing requirements.

Additional reference:

BDD100K:
https://github.com/bdd100k/bdd100k

Use BDD100K only for suitable supported driving-scene tasks. Do not incorrectly claim that BDD100K provides the same road damage classes as RDD2022.

### Detection requirements

- Upload JPG, JPEG, PNG and WEBP images
- Load compatible YOLO weights
- Perform actual image inference
- Show bounding boxes
- Show class labels
- Show confidence scores
- Count detections by class
- Provide a confidence threshold slider
- Display original and annotated image
- Export detection data to JSON or CSV
- Download annotated image
- Show model version and class definitions
- Report inference processing time

Cache model weights appropriately.

If specialized road damage weights are unavailable, do not substitute generic object-detection weights while falsely labeling their outputs as cracks or potholes.

Instead, preserve a working generic detector only if clearly labeled, and provide the specialized training/integration workflow.

Ensure that model labels match the trained model's actual class order.

